# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# Trivy vulnerability suppressions (06 §5.5). Every entry is:
#
#   <CVE-ID> exp:YYYY-MM-DD  ## expires: YYYY-MM-DD — one-line reason
#
# `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` is trivy's own syntax — it stops honoring the suppression
# after that date, so the finding comes back on its own rather than staying
# silenced forever. `## expires: ...` restates the same date in a form
# `trivy-fs`'s own CI step also greps for, as a second, tool-independent
# tripwire. A suppression is a snooze, not a decision.

# js-yaml 4.2.0 (CVE-2026-59869, CVE-2026-84375, GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj: YAML
# parser DoS / quadratic-CPU findings) — transitive via
# @hey-api/json-schema-ref-parser <- @hey-api/openapi-ts, a WP 0.5
# (apps/web) build-time OpenAPI codegen devDependency. It never reaches any
# image WP 0.4 builds: docker/api.Dockerfile's `pnpm install --prod
# --filter-prod=@app/api...` excludes devDependencies and `rm -rf apps/web`
# entirely (verified — see docs/evidence/phase-0/wp0.4-container-hardening.md).
# The fix is a lockfile bump, which is out of this package's scope (WP 0.4 may
# not run `pnpm install` or touch dependencies — see justfile/CI collision
# notes); flagged for whichever package owns apps/web's dependency tree.
CVE-2026-59869 exp:2026-10-12 ## expires: 2026-10-12 — js-yaml transitive via @hey-api/openapi-ts (apps/web devDependency, never shipped); needs a lockfile bump outside WP 0.4's scope
CVE-2026-84375 exp:2026-10-12 ## expires: 2026-10-12 — js-yaml transitive via @hey-api/openapi-ts (apps/web devDependency, never shipped); needs a lockfile bump outside WP 0.4's scope
GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj exp:2026-10-12 ## expires: 2026-10-12 — js-yaml transitive via @hey-api/openapi-ts (apps/web devDependency, never shipped); needs a lockfile bump outside WP 0.4's scope

# personalfi/proxy's `usr/bin/caddy` binary (gobinary scanner): stale
# golang.org/x/net, golang.org/x/crypto, golang.org/x/text, gRPC-Go, and Go
# stdlib versions baked into upstream `caddy:2-alpine`'s last compiled
# build — confirmed this is upstream's *current* published image, not a
# stale pin on our side (`docker pull caddy:2-alpine` resolves to the exact
# digest docker/caddy.Dockerfile already pins). docker/caddy.Dockerfile's
# `apk del curl c-ares` + `apk upgrade libssl3 libcrypto3` already removed
# every *Alpine-package* finding this image had (23 of the original 40); what
# remains is compiled into the Go binary itself and needs an upstream
# rebuild, not an apk operation. Renovate watches this image's digest — a
# fixed upstream build becomes a routine bump. Short expiry (2 weeks, not the
# usual month) because this is upstream's problem to fix, and it should not
# quietly become permanent.
# Re-checked 2026-09-26 (the first expiry): upstream's then-current digest
# (caddy:2-alpine@sha256:6aeddd44c3078b0f9a35206472a11420648a79c184603ef95957d0a20044cb2b,
# v2.11.4) still carries all 17 findings under trivy 0.74.0, so a pin bump
# would not clear them. Snoozed a second two weeks. If the third expiry
# finds upstream still unfixed, the answer is building caddy from source
# with a current Go toolchain (xcaddy) in docker/caddy.Dockerfile — a
# Phase 9 hardening item, not a snooze.
CVE-2026-27145 exp:2026-10-10 ## expires: 2026-10-10 — stale Go stdlib in upstream caddy:2-alpine's compiled binary; needs an upstream image rebuild
